number of on-road research studies worldwide.” In some cases, it may be infeasible or expensive to obtain real-world data under naturalistic studies. Instead, ‘‘opportunistic” data, designated to describe retrospective data collected under real-world driving without supervision of the research team, can be an alternative. In the digital era, with the progress of the Internet and other communication systems, many possibilities have opened up, contributing to the development of new transportation data sources. The simple and constant use of smartphones, including localization systems, social media, or apps, provide the opportunity to obtain additional information alongside traditional sources. In this kind of data, the quantity and quality, as well as the space and time distribution of the information are changing all the time. These dynamic and opportunistic data depend on users’ loyalty and it cannot be controlled or verified, thus making it impossible to evaluate their veracity. Therefore, in order to take advantage of available data and gain relevant knowledge of driver behavior, a large amount of data needs to be collected to ensure interesting and representative results. Transportation studies, including road safety analysis, are investing in this type of data collection and analysis (Blanc & Figliozzi, 2016; Jestico, Nelson, & Winters, 2016; Misra, Gooze, Watkins, Asad, & Dantec, 2014). Crowdsourcing, video sharing, twitter, or other social media are examples of methods used as data sources. For instance, crowdsourced data were used to investigate the underreporting of pedestrian and bicycle crashes (Medury, Grembek, Loukaitou-Sideris, & Shafizadeh, 2017) and to analyze conflicts with bicycles at intersections (Jestico, Nelson, Potter, & Winters, 2017). Furthermore, twitter and other social media were found to be correlated to traffic incidents (Mai & Hranac, 2013; Zhang, Tang, Wang, & Wang, 2015). Additionally, Hawkins and Filtness (2017), who studied driver sleepiness on YouTube, suggested that road safety and public health should consider YouTube and humorous content as a potentially useful dissemination point of educational and interventional material. Nevertheless, to date most of the studies focusing on distraction and drowsiness are based on naturalistic studies and/or driving simulator studies (Papantoniou, 2018; Simmons, Hicks, & Caird, 2016; Wang, Sun, Fang, Fu, & Stipancic, 2017). Although in naturalistic studies, drivers are completely free to decide their trips, both types of studies are controlled experiments, the former in realworld conditions and the latter in a simulated environment, inasmuch the research team designs the data process and the participants consent to be monitored for the purpose of the study. The diversity of study approaches can enrich knowledge about driver behavior as it is a subject in which several factors of different origins (physiological, cognitive, risk perception, road environment, vehicle, etc.) affect the results directly or indirectly, eventually leading to different findings. In the context of emerging data, this study explores ‘opportunistic’ data obtained from a smartphone app that was developed to monitor and alert the driver when he/she becomes distracted and drowsy. Assuming distraction and drowsiness are safety–critical events, risk factors are assessed applying binary logit models separately for distraction and drowsiness. To do this, for each sub-journey (a set of sequential sub-journeys corresponds to a journey), it was established if a drowsiness/distraction event occurred (1 if occurred, 0 otherwise). The risk factors were related to the characteristics of the journey and driver. In the former, variables such as driving time, number of breaks, break duration time, and the first sub-journey were included. Additionally, the driver was characterized by gender and age. The dataset, which included 273 drivers and 634 driving records, was obtained from a commercial DMS. In the next section, a description of the data source and the variables used is provided. The Methodology section presents the models selected to identify
and analyze the risk factors related to the occurrence of drowsiness/distraction events. The Results section shows the results of both models that are explored in depth in the Discussion section. Finally, and due to the ‘opportunistic’ character of the data, the limitations of the study and the closing remarks are clearly provided in the last section.
2. Data description
De-identified data from drivers used in this study were extracted from the DMS vendor database. The DMS was implemented by the company that sells the DMS with the aim to warn the driver rather than develop experimental research. Therefore, the research team that conducted the present study did not supervise the DMS implementation process, nor the data collection and management. Thus, the study explores ‘opportunistic’ data.
2.1. Data source
The DMS vendor is a facial biometric company operating in the automotive sector. The company’s computer-vision system aims to detect drowsiness and distraction states and warns the driver of dangerous driving behavior by sounding an alert of the same kind for both driver states. Two different solutions were developed by the company: a fixed dashboard infrared camera to obtain images of the driver’s face and eyes and a smartphone camera-based application freely available to the general driver population. The first solution was applied to commercial drivers and already analyzed in a different perspective (Ferreira et al., 2019). In contrast, the data from the app solution comprises all kinds of drivers. The DMS solutions used advanced techniques such as deep learning for eye state analysis and a face detection algorithm, and developments were considered for face alignment, performance with glasses, and in bad light conditions. The DMS classifies alerts as distraction or drowsiness without distinguishing the type of distraction (e.g., texting or looking outside) or drowsiness (e.g., microsleeps or fatigue mitigation). The company conducted tests by collecting videos of different subjects driving at different times of the day, in different driving situations (highway and city driving), with and without glasses. The videos were observed and the events (i.e., alerts) were verified using defined heuristics. The company indicates that the face monitoring system used in the overall system (distraction and drowsiness) has an accuracy of 93% (percentage of time the system performs well); for monitoring the eye state to detect sleepiness the company indicates an accuracy of 93% (percentage of times that correctly captured closed eyes); for monitoring the gaze to detect distraction the company indicates an accuracy of 92%. Note that tests were conducted in different conditions (night and day, different positions of the camera, with and without glasses), leading to different values, however, with a minimum value of 78.04%, for the accuracy of the eye state. The system at the beginning and the end of each driving activity (i.e., a trip) registers a timestamp and a global position system (GPS) position. During the activity, the system emits alerts to the driver, conforming to his/her responses, and registers a series of data related to the event, such as a timestamp, a GPS position, the instant speed of the vehicle, and the type of alert (drowsiness or distraction). The data obtained from the app solution also included the driver’s age and sex as registered by the driver. As mentioned in Ferreira et al. (2019), data treatment and rearrangement were carried out. In order to pursue the study goals, the alerts were aggregated by journey or sub-journey, and because of that, not all of the described data were used (e.g., instant vehicle speed, GPS alert).
