The collected data used in this study cover the period from December 2015 to September 2016. The dataset includes 273 drivers and 634 driving records after rearranging the data to aggregate the alerts by driving records, fitting the purpose of the study. Note that the driving times less than 10 min were removed (374 cases) from the original dataset because these kinds of journey (i.e., short journeys) do not fit the purpose of the study.
2.2. Description of variables
In the studied dataset, each observation of driving records corresponds to a potential, partial journey called sub-journey. The journey may include one or more sub-journeys depending on the number of breaks. A driver ID, as well as start and end timestamps are assigned for each sub-journey. Note that the sub-journey is characterized by the driving time instead of the traveled distance because time depends on both the distance and vehicle speed, and thus, in our opinion, it represents the effect of the journey better on the drive ́rs behavior. On the other hand, travel time is the measure usually used in past studies, thus enabling us to compare the results, particularly with the results obtained for professional drivers in (Ferreira et al., 2019). A set of sequential sub-journeys, which correspond to the same driver, is called journey. The time between sub-journeys of the same journey is the breaking duration time. In some cases, there were high values of times between sub-journeys, suggesting that they could represent the end of the journey and the beginning of another new journey. Therefore, considering that there is no additional information about the journey, a threshold is needed to distinguish different journeys, which may include one or more subjourneys, for the same driver. This threshold was set as the limited time interval between two consecutive sub-journeys of 9 h. This time value was based on the EC Regulation No 561/2006 from the European Parliament and the Council of 15 March, 2006 for the harmonization of certain social legislation relating to road transport, which establishes a minimum daily rest period of 9 h for professional drivers (Ferreira et al., 2019). Nevertheless, other threshold values such as 6, 8, and 10 h were tested, with no significant differences in the results. Additionally, this threshold was the same applied in Ferreira et al. (2019), and therefore, allows a comparison between the results of the studies. In order to study distraction and drowsiness distinctly, two datasets were established by separating both types of alerts. The following variables were selected and/or created for both datasets bearing in mind that the dataset observation is a sub-journey:
i. Sub-journey time in hours – driving time between breaks; ii. Journey time in hours – the cumulative driving time up to the observed sub-journey; iii. First sub-journey – dummy variable (0/1) in which the subjourney is classified as 1 if it is the first of a set of subjourneys (or the only one) and 0 otherwise; iv. Number of breaks – the number of breaks previous to the observed sub-journey; v. Breaking duration time in hours – the duration of the last break (time between sub-journeys) before the observed sub-journey; vi. Age – age of the driver classified by one of the following groups: 0 if between 18 and 65; 1 if equal or more than 65; 2 if unknown; vii. Gender – gender of the driver classified by one of the following groups: 0 if male, 1 if female and 2 if unknown.
Note that for the first sub-journey or for a journey without a break, the breaking duration time and the number of breaks are
equal to zero. Moreover, other age groups were analyzed, however, no statistical significance was found after running the models. The database is made up of a total of 634 sub-journeys which correspond to 381 journeys made by 273 drivers. As can be observed in Table 1, the sub-journey has an average time length of 0.40 h (i.e., 24 min) for a total of 252.4 driving hours. A typical journey lasts on average 0.66 h, which is not much more than the average sub-journey time. In fact, a journey has 1.9 subjourneys on average. The number of breaks per journey was less than 1 (see Table 1). Indeed, a significant number of the journeys (40%) were made without any breaks (i.e., with only one subjourney). Moreover, the driver stopped around 33 min on average between driving times. Regarding the categorical variables, the first sub-journey variable was included to eventually represent a different driver behavior in terms of inattention assuming that the driver, when starting to drive, was likely to be focused on the driving task. As shown in Table 1, 60% of the sub-journeys comprised the first sub-journey. Additionally, two categorical variables were included to represent the age and gender of the driver for each sub-journey. As can be seen in Table 1, most of the sub-journeys were driven by men (88% vs. 8% of women) aged between 18 and 65 years-old (77% vs. 9% were above 65 years-old). Finally, in 4% and 14% of the sub-journeys, there was no information about the driver’s gender and age, respectively. Regarding the alert events, in 13% of the sub-journeys, at least one alert of drowsiness was registered. In addition, in 52% of the sub-journeys, at least one alert of distraction was recorded. It can be concluded that most of the sub-journeys were driven without any driver drowsiness event, but in contrast in almost half of the sub-journeys a distraction event was detected.
3. Methodology
A binary model was used to assess the relationship between key factors and the probability associated with the occurrence of a drowsiness/distraction event. Considering a binary response and the aforementioned variables, which are gathered in vector, x, the probabilities are represented as follows:
