that the driver’s age is not associated to the probability of a distraction event occurring, however drivers that did not provide the gender information are prone to distraction events occurring.
5. Discussion
This study explores ‘opportunistic’ data, which were obtained from a DMS available to any driver owning a smartphone, in order to analyze factors that influence the probability of a drowsiness and distraction event occurring. The studied factors are related to the journey and driver’s characteristics. Despite the fact that various studies have been developed to study driver behavior focusing on drowsiness and distraction, to the best of our knowledge, there are no similar studies to the present one. Additionally, most of the past studies used a driver monitoring system that recorded the driver state but did not alert the driver, and therefore having no impact on the behavior. As mentioned by Fitzharris, Liu, Stephens, and Lenné (2017), when drowsiness feedback was provided to the driver, the number of events decreased significantly. In fact, when feedback is given to the driver (i.e., sounding an alert in this case), the driver can decide to stop and have a rest or a coffee, for example, and, as such, alters the journey concerning the situation in which there is no feedback. Therefore, no direct comparison of findings from other studies can be provided. Nevertheless, the present findings can be related to conclusions from other studies. In this study, two models were considered to analyze distraction and drowsiness separately. Indeed, the results show some effect differences of the variable on the probabilities of drowsiness and distraction occurring. This is particularly true in the case of variables describing the drivers’ age and gender. The probability of a drowsiness event occurring is highest for older drivers and
