contributions. However, several limitations are associated to this sampling process. The most relevant concern consists of the impossibility of access and observing data origin, resulting in retrospective and uncontrolled information. Additionally, journey schedule and purpose, driver characteristics, or real-time information from the driver or environment (e.g., video recording of driver and surrounding) are examples of missing information that would help to understand the results better. Complementing the lack of relevant information and the difficulty of guaranteeing that the results of ‘‘opportunistic” data have sufficient quality or efficiency, no one can ensure that the results are always associated to the same driver on a single journey. Indeed, Fitzharris et al. (2017) reported the same issue in an experiment with similar technologies. In addition, the used DMS solution has a lack of information associated to the type of distraction events and the level of drowsiness. Distinguishing the nature of diverting attention could have advantages where different types of distraction might exist and be correlated to different effects on the driver’s performance. However, despite the distraction or drowsiness causes, the main effect remains (i.e., safety driving can be affected by the vulnerability to risky situations). Despite these limitations, interesting conclusions can be pointed out based on the present study’s results that may complement other studies. First, and as expected, the driving time is a relevant exposure to the risk variable, which means that by increasing the driving time, the probability of distraction and drowsiness events occurring also increases. Nevertheless, this effect can be highly mitigated if the driver stops during the journey. Although the probabilities of distraction events occurring during the journey is higher than drowsiness events, the impact of increasing the driving time of the journey and stopping during the journey on the probability of drowsiness is higher than the probability of distraction. Overall, the results indicate that the main risk factor of inattention is driving continuously (i.e., without stopping), but it is irrelevant whether the stop is long or short as well as the total time spent on the journey. It should be noted that the journeys’ characteristics and the number of distraction and drowsiness events and therefore, the probabilities, may be different from those analyzed in this study if no feedback is provided to the driver. Regarding the effects of the driver’s gender and age on both probabilities, this study reveals that the elderly are more prone to drowsiness. Interestingly, the effect of age on drowsiness has been widely investigated resulting in somewhat different results depending on the analyzed context (simulated vs. real-world environment, sleep deprivation vs. normal sleep, and so on). In contrast to the results obtained, some studies pointed out that the young people are drivers who are more prone to drowsiness events. Moreover, there is a specific drivers’ group who did not provide gender information, which is associated to drowsiness and distraction with different trends. Finally, the study shows that data gathered by a driver monitoring system has the potential of helping to assess risk factors, leading to the main conclusion that non-professional drivers are exposed to risks on long drives, however without any kind of legal control in contrast to professional drivers.
